EXTRA = [
    {"after": "physiology", "id": "predialysis", "nav": "Before connecting", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Before connecting: when to call first, and the first-ever treatment",
     "html": """
<p>The pre-treatment assessment ends in one decision: start as prescribed, or call the physician before starting. Once the patient is connected, blood is outside the body and anticoagulant has been given. Some problems are far easier to manage if you find them five minutes earlier. The numeric thresholds for blood pressure, temperature, oxygen saturation, and weight gain are those of the unit&rsquo;s policy or the patient&rsquo;s standing orders.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Finding before treatment</th><th>Why it matters before connecting</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fever or chills</td><td>Possible access or other infection; cultures and antibiotics may need to be ordered, and the catheter may be the source</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chest pain, new palpitations, or fainting since the last session</td><td>Possible cardiac ischemia or arrhythmia, which fluid removal can worsen; an electrocardiogram (ECG) and physician review come first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Breathlessness at rest, inability to lie back, or low oxygen saturation</td><td>Usually fluid overload, which dialysis treats, but the patient may need oxygen, a changed prescription, or a monitored setting; infection and cardiac causes are also possible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood pressure above or below the unit&rsquo;s thresholds</td><td>Very low pressure leaves no reserve for fluid removal; very high pressure with headache, visual change, or confusion is an emergency in itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>New confusion, drowsiness, weakness of one side, or slurred speech</td><td>Stroke, low blood sugar, infection, or medicine toxicity; heparin may be dangerous</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access problem: no thrill or bruit, swelling, redness, discharge, a catheter that has moved or has a damaged cuff or line</td><td>The access may be unusable or infected (Module 7)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bleeding of any kind, black stools, a fall, a head injury, surgery, a dental extraction, or a biopsy since the last session, or a procedure planned soon</td><td>The anticoagulation order may need to be reduced or omitted; you ask the question and the physician decides</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight gain far above the patient&rsquo;s usual</td><td>The fluid goal may exceed what can be removed safely in one session; a longer or extra session may be ordered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight at or below target, or recent vomiting, diarrhea, or poor intake</td><td>The patient may be volume depleted or may have lost body mass; removing fluid to the old target causes hypotension</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missed sessions</td><td>High potassium and fluid overload are likely; pre-treatment blood tests or rhythm monitoring may be ordered</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Medicines today.</strong> Ask, by name, what was taken and what was withheld this morning: blood pressure medicines, insulin and other diabetes medicines, anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs, and any new prescription or herbal product. Record the answer. It explains much of what happens in the next four hours.</p>
<p><strong>The first-ever treatment and dialysis disequilibrium syndrome.</strong> Follow the chain. In a person with very high urea who has never been dialyzed, urea is equally high in blood and in brain tissue. Dialysis clears urea from the blood quickly. Urea leaves brain cells more slowly. For a time the brain holds more dissolved particles than the blood, and water moves into it by osmosis. The brain swells. Rapid correction of acidosis may contribute.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Who is at risk:</strong> first and early treatments, very high urea, children, older adults, people with existing brain disease such as a previous stroke or seizures, and people who return after missing many sessions.</li>
<li><strong>How it is prevented:</strong> the first sessions are deliberately gentle, as prescribed: short, with a low blood flow rate and often a smaller dialyzer, repeated over consecutive days so that urea falls in steps. Your part is to deliver exactly that. Running a first session longer or faster to &ldquo;clean the patient well&rdquo; is the error that causes the syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>How it presents:</strong> usually in the second half of treatment or in the hours after it. Headache, nausea and vomiting, restlessness, blurred vision, and muscle cramps come first. Disorientation, tremor, seizures, and coma follow in severe cases.</li>
<li><strong>What you do:</strong> treat new headache with restlessness or confusion in a new patient as disequilibrium until shown otherwise. Slow or stop the treatment as the unit protocol directs. Keep the patient safe from falls and aspiration. Check blood pressure and blood sugar, and notify the physician at once. Stroke, low blood sugar, low sodium, and severe hypertension can look the same, and are considered together. Medicines to reduce brain swelling are given only on order.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tell new patients and their companions in advance that the first treatments are short on purpose, and that a headache or a feeling of restlessness should be reported at once, not endured.</p>
<p><strong>Every pre-treatment assessment ends in a decision: connect, or call first. In a first treatment, deliver the gentle prescription exactly as written.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "predialysis", "id": "monitoring", "nav": "Intradialytic symptoms", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Monitoring during treatment: what to look at, and what symptoms mean",
     "html": """
<p>Measure blood pressure and pulse at the interval the unit policy sets. Measure more often when the patient is unstable, has a history of hypotension, or has just had an intervention. A monitoring round is more than recording numbers. Spend about half a minute at each station and look at:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The face and speech.</strong> Color, sweating, yawning, a patient who has gone quiet, or who is harder to rouse than at the last round. Speak to patients who appear asleep. Hypotension and low blood sugar both look like sleep.</li>
<li><strong>The access.</strong> Uncovered and visible, tape intact, needles not displaced, no blood, swelling, or wetness. Catheter connections tight, and limbs not kinked.</li>
<li><strong>The lines and chambers.</strong> No kinks, clamps correct, chamber levels held, no foam or clot. Blood that is darkening evenly may be clotting. Blood that looks translucent or cherry-red suggests hemolysis.</li>
<li><strong>The machine.</strong> Arterial and venous pressures compared with the previous reading, because a trend tells you more than a single value; blood flow as ordered; fluid removed so far against the goal; time remaining.</li>
</ul>
<p>Document at the time of observation. Record a reading that changed, the action you took, and the patient&rsquo;s response.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Common causes</th><th>First nursing response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Muscle cramps</td><td>Fluid removed too fast or below true target weight; hypotension; low sodium or other electrolyte shifts</td><td>Check blood pressure. Reduce or pause ultrafiltration (UF) per protocol; stretch the muscle. Report recurrent cramps: the target weight or the removal rate may need review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nausea, vomiting</td><td>Most often hypotension; also disequilibrium, a reaction, cardiac ischemia, a dialysate problem</td><td>Check blood pressure first. Protect the airway and turn the head. Treat the cause</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headache</td><td>Disequilibrium in new patients; high blood pressure; caffeine withdrawal during a long session; a dialysate problem</td><td>Check blood pressure and neurological state. A severe or sudden headache, or one with confusion or vomiting, is reported at once</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Chest pain (emergency)</strong></td><td>Cardiac ischemia; hypotension; hemolysis; air entry; dialyzer reaction; less often pericarditis or a blood clot in the lung</td><td>Stop UF and reduce blood flow per protocol. Check blood pressure, pulse, and oxygen saturation; look at the blood in the lines and for air. Give oxygen per protocol, obtain an ECG, and call the physician. If hemolysis, air, or a reaction is suspected, follow the first actions for that event</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Palpitations or irregular pulse (urgent)</strong></td><td>Shifts in potassium, calcium, or magnesium; fluid removal; underlying heart disease</td><td>Check pulse and blood pressure; obtain an ECG or rhythm strip per protocol; stop UF if blood pressure is low; notify. With chest pain, breathlessness, or faintness, treat as an emergency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Itching</td><td>Starting within minutes of connection, with flushing or hives: a reaction to the dialyzer, germicide, or a medicine. Longstanding: dry skin, high phosphate, inadequate dialysis</td><td>New itching early in treatment: look for hives, wheeze, and hypotension, and respond as for a reaction. Chronic itch: report for treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Fever or chills (urgent)</strong></td><td>Catheter-related bloodstream infection; other infection; pyrogenic reaction from water, dialysate, or a reprocessed dialyzer</td><td>Measure temperature and blood pressure; examine the access. Notify the physician; cultures and antibiotics are by order. A second patient with the same symptom triggers the cluster rule (Module 8)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood pressure rising during treatment</td><td>Fluid overload that has not yet been corrected; removal of blood pressure medicines by dialysis; sodium gain from dialysate; pain or anxiety</td><td>Recheck after rest; ask about headache, visual change, and chest pain. Report a repeated pattern; it is associated with worse outcomes in observational data and usually leads to reassessment of target weight and medicines[[flythe2020]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low blood sugar in a person with diabetes</td><td>Insulin or tablets taken without the usual meal; reduced insulin clearance in kidney failure; glucose lost to dialysate if a glucose-free dialysate is used</td><td>Sweating, tremor, hunger, confusion, or unexplained drowsiness: check capillary glucose immediately and treat per protocol. Do not assume hypotension</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Restlessness or new confusion (emergency until explained)</strong></td><td>Hypotension; low blood sugar; low oxygen; disequilibrium; stroke; sepsis; medicine accumulation</td><td>Check blood pressure, glucose, and oxygen saturation; stop UF if pressure is low; keep the patient safe; call the physician</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Two habits protect against the commonest errors. First, measure blood pressure and blood sugar before you put any symptom down to anxiety or tiredness. Second, when a symptom does not behave as expected after your first response, widen the list of causes and call early. Hypotension that does not correct promptly is not ordinary intradialytic hypotension (IDH).</p>
<p><strong>A patient who looks asleep may be hypotensive or hypoglycemic. Wake them, measure, and only then decide.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "evidence", "id": "idh-prevention", "nav": "Preventing hypotension", "tag": "Evidence", "h2": "Preventing intradialytic hypotension: strategies and the reason for each",
     "html": """
<p>Treating an episode protects the patient today. Preventing the next one means changing something in the prescription or in the days between treatments. Each strategy below follows from the mechanism. Blood volume falls when fluid is removed faster than it refills from the tissues. Pressure falls when the heart and vessels cannot compensate.[[kanbay2020]][[flythe2020]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Physiological reason</th><th>Your part</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Reassess the target weight</td><td>If the patient has gained flesh weight, or the target was set too low, every session tries to remove fluid that is not excess</td><td>Report cramps, post-treatment dizziness, and washed-out fatigue; note improved appetite or loose clothing; never chase a target that the patient cannot tolerate without asking for review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limit weight gain between sessions, mainly by restricting salt</td><td>Less to remove means a lower hourly rate. Thirst follows sodium, so salt restriction works where fluid instructions alone fail</td><td>Teach in food terms: fish sauce, soy sauce, bouillon cubes, instant noodles, dried and canned foods</td></tr>
<tr><td>Longer or additional sessions</td><td>The same volume removed over more hours lowers the rate and gives tissue fluid time to refill the circulation. Higher hourly rates are associated with higher mortality in observational data[[flythe2011]]</td><td>Protect the prescribed time: start on time, minimize interruptions, discourage early sign-off, and document every shortened session and the reason</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cooler dialysate</td><td>Warm dialysate dilates skin vessels; a slightly cooler one promotes constriction and supports pressure</td><td>Set the temperature only as ordered; offer a blanket; report shivering or discomfort</td></tr>
<tr><td>Avoid meals during treatment in patients prone to hypotension</td><td>Digestion diverts blood to the gut and lowers vascular resistance</td><td>Advise a meal before arrival or after treatment; apply the unit policy on eating; people with diabetes need an individual plan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review the timing of blood pressure medicines</td><td>A dose taken just before treatment blunts the vessel and heart-rate response to fluid removal</td><td>Record what was taken; relay the pattern. The prescriber changes timing; the patient is told not to stop medicines on personal initiative</td></tr>
<tr><td>Treat anemia</td><td>Low hemoglobin reduces oxygen delivery, so the heart tolerates low pressure poorly</td><td>Give the ordered agents and iron; report hemoglobin outside the range</td></tr>
<tr><td>Look for heart disease and other causes</td><td>A weak or stiff heart, valve disease, arrhythmia, pericardial fluid, and autonomic neuropathy all limit compensation. A new pattern of hypotension can be the first sign of infection or a cardiac event</td><td>Report a change from the patient&rsquo;s usual pattern promptly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review dialysate composition</td><td>Dialysate sodium, calcium, and potassium influence vascular tone, heart contraction, and rhythm</td><td>Confirm the prescribed concentrate is the one connected</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>How strong is the evidence?</strong> Most of these measures rest on physiology, observational studies, and small trials that measured blood pressure during treatment, which is a surrogate, not survival.[[flythe2020]]</p>
<p><em>Fluid removal rate.</em> In a secondary analysis of 1,846 patients from the Hemodialysis Study, a randomized trial, patients whose removal rate was over 13 mL per kg per hour had higher all-cause mortality than those at 10 mL per kg per hour or less (adjusted hazard ratio 1.59).[[flythe2011]] The trial did not randomize patients to removal rates, so this is an observational finding. Sicker or heavier-gaining patients may need faster removal, and that alone could explain part of the link. It does not prove that slowing removal saves lives.</p>
<p><em>Cooler dialysate.</em> Small studies showed fewer hypotensive episodes with cooler dialysate. The MyTEMP trial then tested it as a center-wide policy. It randomized 84 centers in Ontario, Canada, treating 15,413 patients. Cardiovascular death or admission occurred in 21.4% of patients in the cooler group and 22.4% in the standard group, an adjusted hazard ratio of 1.00. The mean fall in systolic pressure during sessions did not differ.[[mytemp2022]] Cooling is therefore an individual prescription for patients who benefit and tolerate it, not a routine setting.</p>
<p><em>Sodium profiling and monitoring.</em> Routine sodium profiling, in which dialysate sodium starts high and falls during the session, can reduce symptoms on the day. It tends to leave the patient with a net sodium gain, more thirst, larger weight gain, and higher blood pressure before the next session. The KDIGO conference report does not support it as a routine measure.[[flythe2020]] Machine-based blood-volume monitoring has not been shown to improve outcomes consistently.[[flythe2020]]</p>
<p><strong>Pattern recognition is nursing work.</strong> The physician sees the patient for a few minutes. You see every session. A patient whose pressure falls in the same hour of every treatment has a prescription problem, not bad luck. Report it in a form that can be acted on: how many of the last sessions were affected, at what time into treatment, at what weight gain and hourly removal rate, what was taken or eaten beforehand, what intervention was needed, and the weight on leaving compared with target. A unit that records episodes consistently can also review them at its quality meeting.</p>
<p><strong>One episode needs treatment. A pattern needs a report, with the numbers that let the nephrologist change the plan.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "bedside", "id": "ending", "nav": "Ending treatment", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Ending treatment, discharge, and handoff",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Stopping at the right time.</strong> Treatment ends when the prescribed time has been delivered. It does not end when the fluid goal is reached, or when the shift is running late. Make up time lost to alarms and bypass where the schedule and the order allow, or report it. If a patient asks to stop early, explain plainly what shortened treatment costs. Document the request, the time delivered, and the explanation you gave.</p>
<p><strong>Returning the blood.</strong> Return the blood in the circuit with saline, following the machine and unit procedure. Never use air to push blood back, because nothing then stands between the air and the patient. The air detector and venous clamp stay active throughout. Stay at the station, watch the venous line, and stop the return as soon as the line is acceptably clear. Count the saline given in the fluid balance. Do not return the blood when hemolysis or a severe reaction is suspected, when air is in the venous line, or when the circuit is clotted, as the earlier table explains.</p>
<p><strong>Fistula or graft.</strong> Withdraw each needle completely, at the angle it went in, before you apply pressure. Pressing while the needle is still in the vessel cuts the wall. Press with two fingers over gauze, at the skin puncture and just above it where the vessel was entered. Press firmly enough to stop bleeding, and lightly enough that you can still feel the thrill beyond your fingers. Hold without lifting to look, for the time the unit procedure states. Use clamps only where policy allows, and never on an access with a weak thrill. After dressing, confirm the thrill and bruit. Report bleeding that takes much longer than this patient&rsquo;s usual. The causes include too much anticoagulant, a narrowing downstream that raises pressure in the access, and a needle site used too often.</p>
<p><strong>Catheter.</strong> Flush each lumen and then fill it with the locking solution, in the volume printed on that lumen, as ordered. Close the clamps before removing the syringe, and apply new sterile caps with the aseptic technique of Module 7. Check the exit-site dressing, and change it if wet, loose, or soiled.</p>
<p><strong>Post-treatment assessment and discharge.</strong> Take blood pressure and pulse sitting and then standing. Weigh the patient and compare with the target and with the fluid removed. Check the needle sites or catheter again. Ask about symptoms directly. The patient is ready to leave when all of the following hold:</p>
<ul>
<li>Blood pressure is within the patient&rsquo;s acceptable range without a significant fall or dizziness on standing.</li>
<li>Needle sites are dry under the dressing, with thrill present, or the catheter is capped, clamped, and dressed.</li>
<li>There is no chest pain, breathlessness, new confusion, or active bleeding.</li>
<li>The patient can walk as usual, or the usual escort is present.</li>
<li>A person with diabetes has had blood sugar checked where indicated and has food available.</li>
</ul>
<p>A patient who does not meet these stays for observation, and you tell the physician. Report a weight well above or below target at the end. Do not simply record it.</p>
<p><strong>Teaching before the patient leaves.</strong> Keep the dressing on for the time the unit advises. If a needle site bleeds at home, press with clean gauze or cloth, raise the arm, and come back or go to an emergency department if it does not stop. Feel the thrill daily and report its loss the same day. Do not allow blood pressure measurement, blood draws, or heavy carrying on the access arm. Expect to be tired. Dizziness on standing, chest pain, breathlessness, fever, or bleeding are not expected and need a call. Confirm the next session, and the salt and fluid plan until then.</p>
<p><strong>Handoff to a ward or another facility.</strong> Inpatients and transferred patients return to staff who did not see the treatment. A structured format such as Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation (SBAR) keeps the content complete. A systematic review of 11 studies, eight of them before-and-after comparisons, found moderate evidence that SBAR improves patient safety, mostly in telephone calls from nurses to physicians, and noted a lack of high-quality research.[[muller2018]] For a dialysis handoff the content is:</p>
<ul>
<li>Time delivered against prescribed, fluid removed against goal, weight before and after.</li>
<li>Vital signs at the end and any events during treatment, with what was done.</li>
<li>Anticoagulant given and when, so that the ward knows the bleeding risk for the next hours and the timing of any procedure.</li>
<li>Medicines given during dialysis, and medicines that were held for dialysis and are now due.</li>
<li>Blood tests drawn, and whether any result is pending or critical.</li>
<li>Access condition, dressing, and catheter lock; an instruction that the dialysis catheter is not to be used for other purposes unless the nephrologist orders it.</li>
<li>What to watch for and when the next treatment is planned.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The session ends when the prescribed time is delivered and the patient meets the discharge criteria, not when the clock or the queue says so.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "ending", "id": "medicines", "nav": "Medicines and dialysis", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Medicines around dialysis: what is removed, what is given, and when",
     "html": """
<p><strong>The principle of removal.</strong> A dialyzer removes a drug the same way it removes urea. Removal is greater when the molecule is small, dissolves in water, travels free in plasma rather than bound to protein, and stays mostly in the bloodstream instead of spreading into tissues. Large, highly protein-bound, or tissue-distributed drugs are removed little. High-flux membranes remove larger molecules than low-flux ones, so the same drug can behave differently in two units. That is why the timing of a dose relative to dialysis is part of the order, and why you confirm it rather than assume it.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Reasoning</th><th>Nursing action</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Medicines that dialysis removes, including many antibiotics</td><td>A dose given shortly before treatment is partly washed out, leaving the patient under-treated</td><td>Give after treatment, or in the last part of the session when the order says so. On a ward, withhold a pre-dialysis dose only if the order says so, and make sure the post-dialysis dose is actually given</td></tr>
<tr><td>Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</td><td>Given at dialysis so that doses are supervised and no extra injection visits are needed</td><td>Check blood pressure and the latest hemoglobin against the order before giving; give by the route ordered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intravenous iron</td><td>Reactions are uncommon but can be severe</td><td>Give at the rate and dilution for that product, with resuscitation equipment at hand; observe during and after the dose; withhold and ask if the patient has an active infection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intravenous vitamin D analogs</td><td>Suppress parathyroid hormone but raise calcium and phosphate</td><td>Check that recent calcium and phosphate results allow the dose under the order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood pressure medicines</td><td>Some are removed by dialysis and some are not; a dose before treatment can contribute to hypotension, and a dose withheld can lead to high pressure afterward</td><td>Follow the individual plan; record what was taken; report hypotension or rising pressure so timing can be reviewed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulin and other diabetes medicines</td><td>Failing kidneys clear insulin slowly, appetite on dialysis days is variable, and meals are displaced by the schedule</td><td>Ask about dose and food today; check glucose per protocol and with any suggestive symptom; keep a fast-acting sugar source at hand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs taken at home</td><td>Their effect adds to the heparin given for the circuit</td><td>Make sure the prescriber of the dialysis anticoagulant knows; watch needle sites longer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phosphate binders, water-soluble vitamins, and other routine medicines</td><td>Binders work only with food; water-soluble vitamins are lost in dialysate</td><td>Reinforce binders with meals and vitamins after treatment as prescribed</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Three checks for any medicine given at the station.</strong> The order states the timing relative to dialysis. You confirm the patient&rsquo;s identity and allergies at the chair, where a patient is easily confused with the person who usually sits there. You record the dose on the treatment sheet and, for inpatients, on the ward medication record, so that it is neither omitted nor given twice. When a ward nurse or a relative asks whether a medicine should be given before dialysis and the order is silent, ask the prescriber or pharmacist. Do not apply a general rule.</p>
<p><strong>The order sets the timing of every dose around dialysis. When it is silent, ask. Never guess.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "medicines", "id": "unit-emergencies", "nav": "Unit emergencies", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Unit-wide emergencies and readiness",
     "html": """
<p>The earlier table dealt with one patient in trouble. Some events affect every station at once. The response then depends on preparation done on ordinary days.</p>
<p><strong>Readiness.</strong> Survival from cardiac arrest depends on immediate chest compressions and early defibrillation. That requires a defibrillator that is present, charged, and familiar.[[aha2025als]] A named person checks the emergency cart and defibrillator at the frequency the unit sets, and signs for it: pads and electrodes in date, battery charged, drugs and airway equipment complete, and the seal intact. Check oxygen cylinders for content and a working regulator. Test suction by turning it on. Licensing rules require current life-support training for nursing staff, as the evidence section notes.[[doh2012]] Assign roles at the start of the shift, before any event: who leads, who compresses, who manages the circuit, who brings the cart, who calls the physician and the ambulance, and who stays with the other patients. A short drill on the unit&rsquo;s own equipment teaches more than a lecture.</p>
<p><strong>Power failure.</strong> Brownouts are part of Philippine practice. When power is lost, the machines alarm, and dialysate flow and heating stop. Many machines have a battery that keeps the blood pump and safety monitors running for a limited time. It does not continue dialysis. The reasoning runs in this order:</p>
<ul>
<li>Go to the patients, not the switchboard. Check each one and each access. Reassure them. A dark, alarming room frightens people.</li>
<li>Find out whether the generator has started and how long the outage is expected to last. The charge nurse and physician decide, under the unit plan, whether to wait or to end treatments. They weigh battery time against the risk of clotting in a circuit that is not moving.</li>
<li>If blood must be returned without power, return it by hand with the pump crank, in the direction and manner the manufacturer describes, using saline. During manual return the air detector and venous clamp may not be protecting the patient. Watch the venous line continuously and stop before any air reaches it. Never return blood through a line that contains air.</li>
<li>Record for every patient the time treatment stopped, time delivered, fluid removed, whether blood was returned, and condition on leaving. Report to the nephrologist so that further treatment can be arranged.</li>
<li>When power returns, do not assume the machines and the water system are ready. Interrupted disinfection cycles are repeated and tested, and the technician checks the water system before treatments resume (Module 8).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Water or dialysate failure during treatment.</strong> If the water supply fails, the loop pressure drops, or a required water test fails while patients are connected, the machines go into bypass or alarm. Blood keeps circulating, but no dialysis occurs. If the supply cannot be restored promptly, end treatments in an orderly way, with blood returned as usual. The exception is suspected contamination with patients showing symptoms. Those patients are managed as the hemolysis and cluster guidance directs, and their blood is not returned.</p>
<p><strong>Fire, earthquake, flood, and evacuation.</strong> When the building must be left within minutes, a normal return of blood for every patient is not possible. The unit plan sets the method. The usual principles are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Each station has an emergency disconnect kit, checked regularly, with the items the unit plan specifies, such as clamps, caps, gauze, and tape.</li>
<li>&ldquo;Clamp and disconnect&rdquo;: stop the blood pump, clamp the access lines and the bloodlines, and separate them. Leave the needles or catheter in place, capped and secured, to be dealt with at the assembly point. The blood in the circuit is sacrificed. Do not pull access needles in a hurry. An uncontrolled fistula bleed in a stairwell is more dangerous than a clamped needle.</li>
<li>Patients who can walk are released first and directed out with a staff member. Those who need help follow with assistance. This clears the room fastest.</li>
<li>During an earthquake, staff and patients protect themselves until the shaking stops. Disconnection follows.</li>
<li>Someone takes the patient list and the emergency equipment. At the assembly point, count the patients, check each access, remove needles with proper hemostasis, and assess each patient.</li>
</ul>
<p>Disaster planning for people on dialysis begins long before the event. Recommendations developed after major disasters emphasize advance preparation by units and patients, including patient education and plans for treatment elsewhere when the home unit cannot operate.[[sever2023]] These are consensus recommendations drawn from experience, not from trials. In a country with typhoons, floods, and earthquakes, each patient should know what to do if the unit is closed: the emergency diet and fluid limits the unit taught, the contact numbers, and the alternative centers. Each should carry a card or copy of the dialysis prescription, medicines, and hepatitis status.</p>
<p><strong>After any event.</strong> Once patients are safe, hold a short team debrief the same day: what happened, what went well, what was missing or slow. File an incident report with facts and times, without blame. Replace the equipment used and reseal the cart. The event is reviewed at the quality meeting, where the question is what in the system should change. Follow up staff who were distressed by the event. Report a near miss the same way, such as a needle found half out or a cart found with an expired item. It is the cheapest warning the unit will receive.</p>
<p><strong>In a unit-wide emergency, go to the patients first. The plan, the roles, and the checked equipment must already exist before the lights go out.</strong></p>
"""},
]
